<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Guarco</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1470 CE (approx.) · capital Cerro Azul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A coastal lordship of the Cañete valley that resisted the Incas for years, with its stronghold at Cerro Azul by the Pacific.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lordship of Guarco, or Huarco, emerged in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canete-river">Cañete</a> valley south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> around 1100. It developed around fishing villages and irrigation, with a fortified centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-azul-peru">Cerro Azul</a>, a headland on the Pacific coast, and the fortress of Ungará.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Guarco was ruled by local lords who controlled farmland and fishing. Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-azul-peru">Cerro Azul</a> have shown large compounds used to store dried fish, especially anchovies and sardines, which were traded inland for farm goods. Society seems to have been organised around elite families who controlled food stores and labour.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-cieza-de-leon">Pedro Cieza de León</a>, Guarco resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> for several years in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</a>. After its fall around 1470 the Incas built the fortress and administrative centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incawasi">Incawasi</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canete-river">Cañete</a> valley. The region came under Spanish rule in the 1530s.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1100 CE – 1470 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.59%;width:7.36%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1470 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cerro Azul</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Local cults</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Huarco, Señorío de Guarco</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1285&amp;focus=guarco" aria-label="Guarco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2582 2663 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Guarco lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2902 2919L3027 2937"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3027 2937h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2902 2919h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2902 2919h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cerro Azul. <a href="/atlas?year=1285&amp;focus=guarco">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

